From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SplitPlay <splitplay@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 13:01:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] splitplay: apply the desk profile's stream settings (welcome,
 set_profile)

The Profile object (docs/contracts/ipc.md) carries fec_percent, min_fps,
dpb_frames and pacing_mbps, but the worker only logged the initial
profile and answered set_profile with "unsupported". Upstream has a
config option for two of them (fec_percentage, minimum_fps_target), reads
both once per stream, and hardcodes the other two: send pacing is 80 % of
1 Gbit/s in videoBroadcastThread (stream.cpp, ratecontrol_packets_in_1ms)
and the NVENC DPB is 5 frames for H.264/HEVC and 8 for AV1
(nvenc_base.cpp, configure_reference_frames).

New module src/splitplay/profile.{h,cpp}: one atomic per setting, unset
meaning upstream's behaviour, no JSON (the NVENC object libraries include
it). The streaming code reads it where upstream reads its value:

| Field       | Where                                   | Takes effect |
|-------------|-----------------------------------------|--------------|
| fec_percent | videoBroadcastThread, every frame       | next frame   |
|             | RTSP launch: bitrate allowance for FEC  | next stream  |
| min_fps     | encode_run, every frame (max_frametime) | next frame   |
| pacing_mbps | videoBroadcastThread, every frame       | next frame   |
| dpb_frames  | NVENC configure_reference_frames        | next stream  |

- min_fps is capped at the stream's frame rate; unset keeps upstream's
  rule (minimum_fps_target, otherwise half the frame rate, so an idle
  500 Hz desk re-encodes 250 frames per second). A change is logged by
  the encode loop.
- pacing_mbps: at least one packet per millisecond.
- dpb_frames applies only when the client leaves the reference frame
  count to the host: Moonlight sends maxNumReferenceFrames=0 for decoders
  that support reference frame invalidation and 1 for the others, so the
  others keep a single reference. Capped at 16 (H.264, HEVC) and 8 (AV1).
  This reuses upstream's RFI path unchanged: with a larger DPB a lost
  range of up to dpb_frames-1 frames is answered with
  nvEncInvalidateRefFrames instead of an IDR frame (16 frames = 133 ms at
  120 fps, 32 ms at 500 fps; 5 frames were 10 ms at 500 fps). Each
  reference costs one reconstructed surface of VRAM. If NVENC refuses to
  initialise with the profile's DPB, create_encoder logs a warning and
  retries once with the codec default, so the stream never fails over
  it. The encoder banner now shows "dpb=N".

Control client: welcome and set_profile apply the four fields, all or
nothing. Absent keeps the current value, null restores the default; a
value of the wrong type or out of range (fec 0-255, min_fps 1-1000,
dpb 1-16, pacing 1-100000) rejects set_profile with "bad_request"
(welcome: a warning, nothing applied). set_profile answers ok with
{"applied":[...],"next_stream":["dpb_frames"],"not_applied":[...]};
fps, size, bitrate and codec stay Moonlight's choice, and gpu_priority,
gpu_luid and cpu_set are not applied yet.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01J21Rm42dakZVfEnnyh7aQn

